---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { appStoreURL, pageURL, repositoryURL, supportEmail } from '../lib/site';
---
<SiteLayout title="Support — Omnibar for Safari" description="Set up Omnibar, customize your search engines, and get help with the native Safari extension for Mac." section="support">
  <div class="document wrap">
    <header class="document-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A little help</p><h1>Make yourself<br /><em>at home.</em></h1><p>Setup, search settings, and answers to the everyday questions.</p><a class="text-link" href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>Email {supportEmail} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></header>
    <div class="document-body">
      <section id="setup"><h2>Set up Omnibar</h2>
        <p>Omnibar requires macOS 13 or later and Safari.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>{appStoreURL ? <span><a href={appStoreURL}>Download Omnibar from the Mac App Store</a>, then open Omnibar.</span> : <span>Open the Omnibar app on your Mac. {repositoryURL && <span>If you’re building it yourself, follow the <a href={repositoryURL}>instructions in the source repository</a>.</span>}</span>}</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Open Safari Settings</strong>. In Safari’s <strong>Extensions</strong> settings, enable Omnibar.</li>
          <li>Allow website access for the sites where you want Omnibar to show the current address. You can also allow it on all websites.</li>
          <li>Click Omnibar in Safari’s toolbar. If the button is hidden, choose <strong>View → Customize Toolbar</strong> in Safari and add it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Click the toolbar button, type an address or search, and press <kbd>Return</kbd>. Use <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>Return</kbd> for a new tab or <kbd>Escape</kbd> to close the popover.</p>
      </section>

      <section><h2>Why does it ask for website access?</h2><p>Safari requires website access before it supplies a page’s address to an extension. Omnibar uses that permission to show the URL. It does not inject scripts or styles, read page contents, or collect browsing history.</p><p>If you don’t grant access, you can still type a destination or search. Some Safari pages, including the Start Page, may not provide an address.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Can the field stay in the toolbar?</h2><p>Safari’s extension APIs provide a toolbar button that can open a popover. They do not provide a permanently editable address field or another toolbar row. Omnibar shows the current URL while its popover is open.</p><p>Safari’s tab sidebar, Compact tab layout, and <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>L</kbd> shortcut continue to work independently.</p></section>

      <section id="search"><h2>Choose and order search engines</h2><p>The search menu in the popover chooses an engine for the current search. Click the gear button to open <strong>Search Settings</strong>.</p><p>Under <strong>Search Engines</strong>, drag an engine to move it, or use the up and down buttons. <strong>The first engine is the default.</strong> All seventeen built-in engines are available in every region.</p><p>On first use, your Mac’s Region setting puts Naver first in South Korea; Baidu, Sogou, and 360 Search first in China; Yandex first in Russia; Seznam first in Czechia; and Yahoo Japan first in Japan. Other regions start with Google. This uses a local setting, with no location request or network lookup. Later region changes do not replace your saved order.</p><p>Omnibar does not synchronize its selection with Safari’s search engine setting.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Add a custom engine</h2><ol><li>In <strong>Search Settings → Search Engines</strong>, click <strong>Add</strong>.</li><li>Enter a name and an HTTP or HTTPS search URL containing one <code>%s</code> or <code>{'{searchTerms}'}</code> placeholder.</li><li>Save the engine. It moves to the top of your list and becomes the default.</li></ol><p>For example: <code class="breakable">https://example.com/search?q=%s</code>. Select a custom engine and choose <strong>Edit</strong> or <strong>Remove</strong> to change it. Use ordinary search URLs here; custom templates are saved in local preferences and should not contain credentials.</p></section>

      <section id="kagi"><h2>Use Kagi in Private Browsing</h2><p>Regular Kagi searches use your existing Safari sign-in. For private windows, you can optionally save a <a href="https://help.kagi.com/kagi/privacy/private-browser-sessions.html">Kagi private session link</a> in <strong>Search Settings → Kagi Private Browsing</strong>.</p><p>Paste the link into the secure field and click <strong>Save Link</strong>. It is stored in the local macOS Keychain, is not displayed again, and can be deleted with <strong>Remove Link</strong>. Use this field instead of putting a session token in a custom engine URL.</p><p>Omnibar uses the link only for Kagi searches when Safari confirms that the current page is in Private Browsing. When that information is unavailable, it uses the regular Kagi URL. Direct addresses and other search engines do not read the credential.</p><p>A Kagi session link grants access to your account. Never include it in a support email, screenshot, or public issue. If the link stops working, obtain a new one from Kagi and save it again.</p></section>

      <section id="source"><h2>Free and open source</h2><p>Omnibar is free software released under the BSD 2-Clause license. {repositoryURL ? <span>You can <a href={repositoryURL}>read the source, build it, or report an issue on GitHub</a>.</span> : <span>Contact support for source and build information.</span>}</p><p>The app requires no Omnibar account or subscription. A search provider may have its own account requirements or pricing.</p></section>

      <section class="contact-section"><h2>Still need a hand?</h2><p>Email <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>. Include your macOS and Safari versions, what you expected to happen, and what happened instead. Please remove personal URLs and credentials from screenshots.</p><p>You can read how the app handles information in the <a href={pageURL('privacy')}>privacy policy</a>.</p></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
